.TH DEMANDE 1 "2026-08-19" "crt" "Ecosystem verbs"
.SH NAME
demande \- ask Zach a question over WhatsApp and read his reply
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B demande ask
.I question
.RI [ from-agent ]
.br
.B demande check
.I ticket-id
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B demande
is the client half of zaxon, crt's WhatsApp relay. It is the only channel that
carries a question from an unattended agent to a human, so every other failure
in the estate is meant to reach Zach through this.
.PP
.B ask
sends the question and prints a ticket id immediately \- it does
.B not
wait. A human reply takes minutes to hours.
.B check
polls that ticket; a
.I pending
status is exit 0, because Zach not having answered yet is a normal state and
not a broken promise.
.SH ENDPOINT
The relay is tried at
.I http://127.0.0.1:8643/mcp
then
.IR http://100.107.253.56:8643/mcp ,
and the first that completes an MCP handshake wins. That covers dexter itself
(loopback) and mandark and monkey (tailnet) with no per-host configuration.
zaxon is bound to exactly those two addresses and deliberately not to the LAN.
Override with
.BR ZAXON_MCP_URL .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the question was relayed, or the ticket was read
.TP
.B 2
usage error
.TP
.B 5
the relay handshook but would not carry the message
.TP
.B 6
BLIND \- no relay answered. This is "I cannot ask", never "there was nothing
to ask", and the distinction is why it is not exit 0.
.SH NOTES
This verb carries the endpoint and the whole MCP handshake itself and execs
nothing outside its own tree. Verbs install host-wide on machines that hold no
checkout, so a verb that reaches into a clone is broken everywhere it matters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sonne (1),
crt's
.I provision/dexter/zaxon/README.md
